  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1203 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nA 594 dm upper ridge is situated over the Mid-South this morning\nand hot and humid conditions continue. Temperatures are already\nhitting the mid 90s in some spots with dewpoints in the lower to\nmid 70s. A few more locations will hit triple digits this\nafternoon. This ridge will strengthen to 598 dm this weekend as\nthe heat intensifies over the region. The NBM does show a slight\ndrop in dewpoints this weekend, which will likely result in\nsurface temps climbing into the low 100s over more of the area.\nAs the high heat continues day after day with little to no relief\nat night, the heat risk continues to climb. With that in mind, we\nhave decided to upgrade the entire Mid-South to an Extreme Heat\nWarning through Sunday evening. We cannot completely rule out a\ndiurnal shower or storm through this weekend, though the strength\nof the upper ridge will keep almost everyone dry.\n\nStarting early next week, there are some subtle changes that\noccur with the upper pattern. The upper ridge starts to weaken a\ntouch and retrograde a little toward the Southern Plains. A weak\ncold front may drop into the northern parts of the Mid-South on\nMonday and hang around for a couple of days. This may knock\ntemperatures down a few degrees across northern areas and the TN\nRiver Valley and also bring a slight chance of showers and storms\ninto the area.  However, heat headlines will likely still be\nneeded over a significant part of the Mid-South for much of next\nweek, along and south of wherever that front ends up stalling.\n\nA more significant cool down may be on tap for later next week\ninto next weekend as global models are depicting a significant\ntrough developing over the eastern U.S. and northwesterly upper\nflow for the Mid-South. However, there is a large spread in\nguidance by next weekend, so confidence is fairly low, and there\nare some model solutions that favor upper ridging and heat\nhanging on across the Mid-South.",
